WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.564
In this video, we'll work through why discovery work belongs in a sub-agent, not in your main

2
00:00:05.564 --> 00:00:06.940
agent's context window.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
We'll cover what makes discovery so verbose, how the delegation pattern keeps things clean,

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
and the cases where spawning a child agent is worth it versus cases where it's overkill.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Ask an agent to find the authentication flow, and it will read a dozen files you never wanted in your context.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Sub-agent delegation is the pattern that fixes that.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.000
You spawn a child agent to handle the verbose investigation, and it returns only a focused summary to the parent.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.056
Discovery is the activity itself, finding relevant files, documentation, or context inside a large

10
00:00:45.056 --> 00:00:46.928
codebase or knowledgebase.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
And disposable context is the sub-agent's context window.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.618
It holds all the messy intermediate state, every grep result, every dead-end file rid, every

13
00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:58.938
candidate that turned out to be wrong,

14
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
and all of it is discarded the moment the sub-agent returns its answer.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Let's start with why discovery is so naturally verbose.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.000
When you ask an agent to find the authentication flow in a large codebase, it doesn't just open one file.

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
It searches. It reads candidates. It hits dead ends.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Grep results come back with dozens of matches, most of them irrelevant.

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Each file it opens lands in the context window, whether or not it turns out to be useful.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.047
By the time the agent finds the right file, its context holds pages of noise, on top of whatever

21
00:01:34.047 --> 00:01:35.937
task work it was doing before.

22
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.000
That pollution is the core problem delegation solves, and the reason isolating discovery matters so much.

23
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Compare inline discovery to delegated discovery directly.

24
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.000
When the main agent reads files itself, every grep result and every dead-end accumulates in its context.

25
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.349
Useful state competes with noise as the session continues, and the more files the agent touches, the

26
00:02:00.349 --> 00:02:01.936
worse that crowding gets.

27
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
With delegation, the main agent hands the question to a sub-agent.

28
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
The sub-agent investigates, then returns only its finding.

29
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
The main agent acts on that finding without ever seeing the raw reads.

30
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
The verbose intermediate state never crosses the boundary.

31
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:26.000
It lives inside the sub-agent's context, and it disappears when the sub-agent finishes.

32
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The parent context stays clean for the duration of the task.

33
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
The delegation pattern has four concrete steps.

34
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.000
First, the main agent frames a precise discovery question and defines the shape of the answer it needs.

35
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Second, the sub-agent receives that question along with a minimal tool palette.

36
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Third, the sub-agent investigates and produces a structured summary.

37
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Fourth, the main agent reads the summary and continues its work.

38
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Nothing else crosses the boundary.

39
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The sub-agent doesn't return file contents.

40
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
It doesn't return grep output.

41
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
It returns an answer in the shape the main agent asked for.

42
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.349
That shape is called the return contract, and it is what makes the pattern reliable across different

43
00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:13.936
types of discovery tasks.

44
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Three scenarios make delegation clearly worth the overhead.

45
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.962
Code-based surveys, where finding an answer requires reading many file candidates before you can

46
00:03:23.962 --> 00:03:27.937
narrow down to the right one, benefit enormously from isolation.

47
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.082
Dock spelunking through long reference documents or changelogs, where most pages are irrelevant to

48
00:03:34.082 --> 00:03:36.937
the question at hand, is a strong second case.

49
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.118
Multi-step lookups, where you chain several searches before a conclusion emerges and each step

50
00:03:43.118 --> 00:03:47.934
generates its own layer of intermediate results, are the third clear case.

51
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.286
In all three situations, the volume of intermediate state is high enough that isolating it in a

52
00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:56.933
sub-agent pays for the overhead quickly.

53
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Not every lookup justifies spawning a child agent.

54
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Sub-agent delegation adds real overhead in the form of latency, token cost, and coordination work between agents.

55
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
If you already know the file path, read it in line.

56
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:18.000
If the answer lives in a short document with an obvious location, don't spawn a sub-agent to find it.

57
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Single-file lookups are the clearest case where the overhead outweighs the benefit.

58
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.000
There isn't a clean numeric threshold here.

59
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Delegate when the exploration would dump file contents into your context that you won't reference again.

60
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:37.000
The benefit grows as the investigation spans more candidate files and more dead ends.

61
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.000
The delegation trade is straightforward once you see it clearly.

62
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.000
You spend sub-agent cost, and you buy main agent clarity.

63
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.000
The sub-agent run adds latency and token spend.

64
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.923
In exchange, the main agent avoids context pollution and stays focused on the actual task, rather

65
00:04:54.923 --> 00:04:56.938
than drowning in discovery noise.

66
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.917
Whatever comes back is a hypothesis the parent should verify when the stakes are high, because a

67
00:05:02.917 --> 00:05:05.938
sub-agent can miss a file or misread a signature.

68
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
The net benefit still grows as the discovery gets more complex.

69
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
For large investigations across many files, the trade is almost always favorable.

70
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
For trivial lookups, it isn't.

71
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.322
Discovery is verbose by nature, and handled inline, it pollutes the main agent's context until the

72
00:05:24.322 --> 00:05:25.935
useful state gets buried.

73
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.222
Delegating to a sub-agent isolates that verbosity in a disposable context that disappears when the

74
00:05:32.222 --> 00:05:33.936
child returns its findings.

75
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.890
The pattern pays off for complex, multi-file investigations, and when you already know the path, a

76
00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.929
plain inline read is simpler.

77
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

